# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: deferred-aldb-swank — --aldb-swank: reuse a SLIME swank connection as the aldb channel
#+STATUS: DEFERRED (explicitly deferred in the spec, C.2; split out 2026-10-06)
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: feature
#+DEPENDS: debugger-public-interface-and-on-error (closed)
#+BRANCH: feat-aldb-swank

* Context

Split out of =debugger-public-interface-and-on-error= when it was closed
(clautolisp 2.2.218 / alfe 2.2.162): every other part of that ticket is done
-- the clal-* API, the --on-error / --on-interrupt / --on-quit policies,
--debugger-ui, the aldb transports --aldb-listen [HOST:]PORT and --aldb-stdio,
the options shared with alfe, and --on-interrupt on CCL. This is the one item
its Part D (C.2) marked "(Optional, deferred.)":

#+begin_example
--aldb-swank
    (Optional, deferred.) Reuse an existing SLIME swank connection if one
    is present (spec §20.1 allows this). Likely a separate follow-up.
#+end_example

* What it would be

A third aldb transport beside the TCP listener and stdio: when the process
already runs a swank server with a connected SLIME (a developer running
clautolisp inside their Lisp), the aldb RPC (clautolisp/autolisp-debug-ui-emacs,
DN-10) rides on that connection instead of opening its own socket, so
=aldb.el= attaches without =M-x aldb-connect=.

* Open questions (to settle before implementing)

- How the shipped executables would have swank at all: they do not load it.
  The option only makes sense in a development image, or with swank loaded on
  demand (a quicklisp dependency the binaries do not carry today).
- The framing: aldb's line-oriented S-expr RPC versus swank's own
  length-prefixed messages; an =:emacs-rex= wrapper on the Emacs side, or a
  swank contrib.
- Whether alfe forwards it like the other aldb options (the shared spec makes
  that the default), and what the CAD backends do with it (refuse, like
  --aldb-listen).

